              "text": "When asking how much of a percentage mutual fund managers make, the answer depends on whether you mean **the percentage fee charged to the investors (the fund company's cut)** or **the actual percentage of the fund's assets that the individual portfolio manager takes home as personal salary.** \n\n---\n\n### 1. The Fee Percentage Charged to Investors (The Fund's \"Cut\")\nMutual fund companies make money by charging investors a fee calculated as a percentage of **Assets Under Management (AUM)**. This fee is known as the **expense ratio** (which includes the *management fee*). \n\nUnlike hedge funds\u2014which historically charged a \"2 and 20\" fee (2% of assets + 20% of all profits)\u2014mutual funds are heavily regulated. In the United States, the SEC generally prohibits mutual funds from charging asymmetric performance fees (taking a percentage of the profits). Instead, they must charge flat, asset-based fees:\n\n*   **Actively Managed Mutual Funds:** Typically charge a management fee between **0.5% and 1.5%** of AUM annually. The average actively managed equity mutual fund fee is around **0.7% to 1.1%**. \n*   **Passively Managed Mutual Funds (Index Funds):** Because these funds simply track an index (like the S&P 500) and require no active stock-picking, their fees are much lower, typically ranging from **0.02% to 0.20%**.\n\n*Example: If you invest $10,000 in an active mutual fund with a 1% expense ratio, the management company will deduct $100 over the course of the year to cover their operating costs.*\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Percentage the Individual Portfolio Manager Makes (Personal Compensation)\nA common misconception is that the actual human beings managing the funds directly pocket the 1% management fee. In reality, **individual mutual fund managers are salaried employees** of the asset management firm (like Fidelity, Vanguard, or BlackRock) and **do not** receive a direct, flat percentage of the fund's assets or profits.\n\nInstead, their compensation is structured through a combination of salary and performance metrics:\n\n#### How Mutual Fund Managers are Paid:\n1.  **Base Salary:** Typically ranges from **$150,000 to $1,000,000+** depending on the size of the firm and the manager's experience.\n2.  **Performance Bonus (The largest portion):** Roughly 80% of active mutual fund managers have bonuses tied directly to performance. This is typically calculated using:\n    *   **Benchmark Performance:** Beat a specific benchmark (like the S&P 500) over a 1-year, 3-year, or 5-year period.\n    *   **AUM Growth:** The bonus increases if the fund's total assets grow, which happens when the manager makes good investments or when new investors pour money into the fund.\n\n#### The Relationship Between Fund Revenue and Manager Pay\nAccording to academic research from the National Bureau of Economic Research (NBER) and the Stockholm School of Economics, there is a **concave relationship** between a fund\u2019s revenue and the manager's pay:\n*   A **1% increase** in the total fees a fund generates only increases the individual manager's personal pay by about **0.15%**.\n*   The overwhelming majority of the management fee is kept by the parent company to pay for overhead, analysts, marketing, compliance, and corporate profit.\n\n#### What is their actual take-home pay?\nBecause of this structure, a portfolio manager's personal earnings represent a tiny fraction of a percent of the total fund they manage:\n*   **Median US Fund Manager Pay:** Around **$462,000** per year (base salary + bonus).\n*   **Average (Mean) US Fund Manager Pay:** Around **$1.1 million**. (This figure is skewed higher by top-tier managers at massive funds, who can make **$4 million to $10+ million** annually during highly successful years).\n\n*Example: If a portfolio manager runs a highly successful $2 billion mid-cap mutual fund that charges a 1% fee ($20 million in annual fee revenue), the manager does not get that $20 million. Instead, their salary and performance bonus might total $1.5 million to $2 million. Personally, they are earning roughly **0.07% to 0.10%** of the assets they manage.*",
